The eval report's "In the product" section tracks each of these and flips when the change lands.

Known limits, documented:
- caps, holds and the course cache live in memory per server instance (ponytail comments) until the database lands; on Vercel, caps hold per instance and cache hits will be rare;
- the address ceiling runs on the server's UTC day, so for US families it lifts in the evening, sooner than the "tomorrow" it promises;
- a request refused after the gate (rate limit, bad body) keeps its held turn for up to 2 minutes;
- holds stop concurrent requests from slipping under the turn caps, but cost can still overshoot by the lesson or reply already in flight.

Name scrubbing is still a heuristic:
- an everyday-word or two-letter name typed in lowercase passes through on purpose ("will", "leo");
- a capitalized everyday word at the start of a sentence is scrubbed ("Al final…" for a child called Al);
- contractions on a name ("Ada'll") are not scrubbed;
- the everyday-word list is finite.
